            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"Faith is primarily understood as trusting reliance and confident assurance in God and His promises, often distinguished conceptually from works or obedience, especially in Protestant traditions regarding justification.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Faith is defined as trusting faithfulness—a reliance on God's promises that is inherently maintained and brought to life through obedience.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Trusting reliance on God and His promises.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Trusting faithfulness and reliance on God integrated with obedience.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus commonly distinguishes faith (as trust) from the works or obedience it produces. The book integrates obedience directly into the core definition of faith as 'trusting faithfulness,' rejecting the strict conceptual separation often found in the 'sole instrument' paradigm of justification.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"There is no direct material contradiction of the core referent (trust in God), but the book explicitly adds obedience as a constitutive element of faith rather than merely its result.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book preserves the primary consensus referent (reliance on God's unseen promises) but refines it by elevating 'faithfulness' and 'obedience'—noted as alternatives or secondary aspects in the consensus—into the primary definition.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"Romans 4:4-5 explicitly contrasts faith with works ('to him that worketh not, but believeth...'), suggesting that integrating obedience directly into the definition of faith might obscure the Pauline distinction between faith and works in justification.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
